# Editorial estimates. build and recog are unconditional; law, money, hold assume people are already there.
ODDS = {
"mars-and-beyond": (2,40,60,35,.2,"Nobody has been there, and a private settlement within 25 years would need Starship-class transport that does not yet carry people. Distance helps once you arrive: no navy can reach you in under months. It still launches under a licence from a state, and Outer Space Treaty Art. VI and VIII keep that state responsible. The 2020 Starlink clause calling Mars a free planet has never been tested and no state accepts it."),
"asteroids": (1,40,60,30,.1,"No one has lived on one. Companies built to mine them, Planetary Resources and Deep Space Industries, ended in 2018 and 2019 for lack of money. US, Luxembourg, UAE and Japanese laws let you own what you extract, and none lets you own the rock."),
"moon": (5,25,60,20,.2,"The ban on national ownership in Art. II leaves no route to title. Artemis Accords signatories treat safety zones as allowed, so whoever arrives first with a state behind them shapes the ground, and the same licensing state answers for you. Deeds sold since 1980 have no legal effect."),
"lagrange-cislunar": (3,30,60,20,.1,"Same law as low orbit with worse logistics. The L5 Society's O'Neill colony plans never reached funding, and no private habitat has been built there."),
"geo": (3,25,60,15,.1,"Slots and frequencies go through the ITU, and only member states can file. Habitats would sit in a crowded belt with radiation worse than low orbit. Tonga's filings show that a state can rent slots out; no private group has held one without a state."),
"leo": (25,15,70,10,.2,"Private stations are coming, so getting people there long term is plausible, though no private station has yet flown. Every object must sit on a state's registry, and that state keeps jurisdiction over the people on it. Internal charters and tokens are workable. The FCC's $900,000 Swarm settlement shows how licensing reaches satellites launched abroad."),
"stratosphere-near-space": (5,15,60,10,.1,"No crewed long-stay platform exists. Over land it counts as airspace with no agreed ceiling, and in February 2023 US fighters shot down a Chinese balloon at about 60,000 feet."),
"airspace-over-high-seas": (5,15,60,15,.1,"No state owns this air, but any aircraft needs a state registration and must follow ICAO rules over the high seas. Fuel and maintenance force landings in a country. ADIZ practice shows states also ask for identification well outside their own airspace."),
"airspace-over-states": (20,5,40,2,.1,"Chicago Convention Art. 1 gives the country below complete and exclusive sovereignty. You can fly there with its permission, and it can order you down."),
"ships-flag": (90,50,70,60,.2,"This already works: The World has run as a private community at sea since 2002. Villa Vie, a cheaper attempt, was laid up in October 2026 when the money ran out. You keep such a place by accepting a flag, so the flag state's law applies on board and it is the outside authority. The high score measures living at sea, and independence is what you give up to get it. Without a flag you are in the row for the high seas."),
"high-seas": (35,50,75,15,.5,"Nobody owns it, so no coastal state has title. But a stateless vessel can be boarded under UNCLOS Art. 110, and Art. 60 and Art. 89 give an artificial island no territory of its own. Open-ocean engineering costs from about $50 million for a small platform up to billions, and waves broke the Aisland barge in 2024."),
"central-arctic-ocean-and-svalbard": (5,40,60,15,.2,"Same law as other high seas, with moving ice and no local supply. Svalbard's 1920 treaty gives foreigners residence and trade rights, under Norwegian sovereignty. US v Escamilla shows a court keeping jurisdiction even over a drifting ice island."),
"eez-shelf": (40,25,70,5,.2,"Coastal states have the exclusive right to authorise artificial islands here under UNCLOS Art. 60. The Thai navy raided a seastead off Phuket in 2019, its occupants say beyond 12 miles, and a police complaint cited a law carrying life in prison or death."),
"territorial-sea": (60,15,50,3,.1,"The coast's full sovereignty applies out to 12 nautical miles. You can build only with permission, which puts you in the row for zones granted by a host. Rose Island sat just outside the older limit and lasted eight weeks after declaring independence."),
"reefs-low-tide-elevations": (25,35,60,8,.5,"Reefs covered at high tide cannot be claimed by occupation, according to the 2016 South China Sea award. Founders tried Minerva, Silver Shoals and Misteriosa; nearby states responded by annexing the feature, sending gunboats or suing."),
"abandoned-platforms": (50,50,70,12,1,"Sealand has lasted since 1967 because it was outside the 3-mile limit at the time, and the UK then chose to leave it alone after 1987 when the limit grew to 12 miles. New platforms do not get that history: inside an EEZ the coastal state's rights apply, and old rigs are subject to removal rules."),
"disputed-islands": (10,20,40,2,.2,"Garrisons of several states hold the Spratlys and Paracels. A private claimant would be walking between navies. Tomas Cloma's 1956 Freedomland ended with Taiwan burning buildings and the Philippines absorbing the claim."),
"undersea-water-column": (8,30,60,15,.2,"Aquarius and similar habitats exist as permitted research stations. The water column follows the surface zone above and the seabed belongs to a shelf state or the ISA, so depth brings no new legal shelter."),
"deep-seabed-area": (3,20,50,15,.1,"UNCLOS Art. 137 bars any claim over the Area, which only the International Seabed Authority administers, and its mining code is still unfinished. Deepsea Ventures' 1974 private claim produced nothing."),
"terra-nullius-land": (20,30,60,10,1,"Unclaimed land exists, but states do not treat it as open to private founding. Bir Tawil has no water source and access runs through Egypt or Sudan. Gornja Siga is policed by Croatia, which arrests entrants. Older gaps such as Moresnet and Cospaia lasted only while the neighbours agreed."),
"antarctica-unclaimed": (5,30,50,10,.2,"Antarctic Treaty Art. IV bars new claims, and the Madrid Protocol bans mining. Nothing physical has been built by any claimant; Westarctica, Flandrensis and Talossa exist on paper. Marie Byrd Land is also among the hardest places on Earth to reach."),
"antarctica-claimed": (5,15,30,5,.1,"Seven states hold frozen claims, and treaty parties control access through permits and shared logistics. Greenpeace ran World Park Base from 1987 to 1992 as a campaign and then removed it. Chile and Argentina have used families and births to mark presence."),
"inside-state": (70,10,50,25,.2,"Easy to declare, and many last for years when the state finds them harmless. Hutt River ran for 50 years and still lost every tax case. The country's law keeps applying to you, whatever your passports say."),
"host-state-carve-outs": (50,65,50,45,1,"The most workable route to your own courts and tax rules. DIFC has run its own common-law courts since 2006 under Dubai law. A later government can reverse the grant: Honduras's Supreme Court struck down ZEDEs in 2024, and Próspera now relies on an investment-treaty claim."),
"unrecognised-state": (3,85,60,45,15,"Places such as Somaliland, Transnistria and Northern Cyprus run real governments, on land someone else claims, usually after war or with a patron army. A private group cannot start here. Israel's December 2025 recognition of Somaliland is the rare recent gain, after 34 years. Artsakh shows the downside: it ended by force in 2023."),
"digital-no-territory": (95,40,70,70,1,"Anyone can found one, and there is no place to raid. It holds no territory, and every member and server remains under the law where it sits. Bitnation faded, and the Network School lost its Malaysian licence in July 2026. The Order of Malta keeps sovereign relations with no land, but that comes from centuries of standing, which a new group cannot copy."),
}
BANDS = [("Beyond Earth",["mars-and-beyond","asteroids","moon","lagrange-cislunar"]),("Orbit",["geo","leo"]),("Air",["stratosphere-near-space","airspace-over-high-seas","airspace-over-states"]),("Sea surface",["ships-flag","high-seas","central-arctic-ocean-and-svalbard","eez-shelf","territorial-sea","reefs-low-tide-elevations","abandoned-platforms","disputed-islands"]),("Under the sea",["undersea-water-column","deep-seabed-area"]),("Land and ice",["terra-nullius-land","antarctica-unclaimed","antarctica-claimed","inside-state","host-state-carve-outs","unrecognised-state"]),("No place",["digital-no-territory"])]
EXTRA_ZONES = [
{"id":"inside-state","name":"Claims inside an existing country","who_owns":"The country whose borders it sits inside. Declaring independence changes nothing in that country's law, and its courts apply its tax and criminal law to you as usual.","rules":[{"cite":"Montevideo Convention Art. 1","says":"A state needs a permanent population, a defined territory, a government and the capacity to enter relations with other states."},{"cite":"Montevideo Convention Art. 3","says":"Political existence of a state does not depend on recognition by others; the practical tests still need to be met on land another state does not hold."}],"hook":"Ordinary tax, planning and criminal law. Hutt River's founder was prosecuted for tax documents and the courts held that declaring secession did not remove him from tax law.","can_you_build":"Buy land or declare on land you already use. Nothing stops the declaration. What stops the state is whether you cost it anything.","facts":[],"sources":[{"t":"Montevideo Convention on the Rights and Duties of States (text)","u":"https://www.ilsa.org/Jessup/Jessup15/Montevideo%20Convention.pdf"}]},
{"id":"unrecognised-state","name":"Unrecognised states on land someone else claims","who_owns":"Disputed. A recognised state holds legal title, while a local government actually runs the place, usually after a war or with a patron state's army.","rules":[{"cite":"Montevideo Convention Art. 1","says":"The four practical tests of statehood. Somaliland is often cited as meeting them while lacking recognition."},{"cite":"UN Security Council Res. 541 (1983)","says":"Called Northern Cyprus's declaration legally invalid and asked UN members not to recognise it, an example of how the Council can keep a place out."}],"hook":"Force by the recognised state, as in Artsakh in 2023, and refusal of recognition by others, which cuts access to treaties, banks and UN bodies.","can_you_build":"Not by purchase. All the current cases came out of collapse, war or foreign protection.","facts":[],"sources":[{"t":"Montevideo Convention on the Rights and Duties of States (text)","u":"https://www.ilsa.org/Jessup/Jessup15/Montevideo%20Convention.pdf"}]},
]
